WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.060
It had been a long dig, expeditions like&nbsp;
this were normally tiring. But with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.800
intense heat of the Egyptian sun bearing&nbsp;
down on them, every minute felt like hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:17.220
and every hour a lifetime. Seth and Kayla were&nbsp;
determined not to quit, they had traveled halfway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.900
around the world to get to this site, and they&nbsp;
weren’t about to let the elements stop them from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.840
uncovering the secrets they were searching for.
The rumors had dated back centuries, stories of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.880
a hidden tomb lost somewhere out in the desert.&nbsp;
And as a likely pair of a budding adventurer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.580
and an accomplished archaeologist, neither one of&nbsp;
them could resist the chance to try and hunt down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:44.100
the tomb. If they could uncover it, they’d have&nbsp;
one of the greatest archaeological finds of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.860
century on their hands, both Kayla and Seth&nbsp;
would be forever remembered as the ones who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:56.700
discovered a supposedly unfindable ancient tomb.
Having found their way to some ruins, the remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:01.200
of a temple that had been all but razed to&nbsp;
the ground, ravaged by the forces of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.240
Kayla and Seth had started searching for the&nbsp;
tomb’s entrance. The reason it had stayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.280
undiscovered for so long was the amount of sand&nbsp;
that had covered it over the centuries, storms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.220
from the desert constantly rearranging the grains&nbsp;
and piling on more. But right now, the skies were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:24.240
clear, and all they could do was keep digging.&nbsp;
Until, at long last, their shovels struck stone.&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.700
Clearing a path to the entrance, Kayla flipped on&nbsp;
a torch as she led her archaeologist friend down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.860
a flight of stairs carved into the stone. Seth&nbsp;
followed as they traverse further and further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:39.780
down, the air getting stale the deeper they&nbsp;
went. The whole while, there was a gnawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:45.000
concern at the back of both their minds, that the&nbsp;
rocky ceiling above could give way at any moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.860
trapping them both underground. But before either&nbsp;
one of them could voice their worry out loud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:55.500
they arrived at a large stone door, left wide&nbsp;
open about twenty meters beneath the surface.&nbsp;

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:01.980
“Is this really it?” Kayla asked in disbelief.
“It might be,” Seth replied, the excitement in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:07.620
his voice like that of a kid on Christmas morning.
Standing before them, beyond the threshold of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:13.500
open entrance, was a chamber of stone. Its&nbsp;
ceiling was secure, about seven meters high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.720
and the walls had been covered in hieroglyphics.&nbsp;
Immediately, Seth shot over to the nearest wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.280
pulling a battered journal out of his bag to&nbsp;
start trying to translate what had been written.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.960
While he was doing that, Kayla’s attention&nbsp;
fell over the rest of the room. For a tomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:33.600
there was a surprising lack of a sarcophagus,&nbsp;
nor any other similar resting place for a body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.920
with no signs that anyone else had&nbsp;
already been here to remove one either.&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.500
All there really was within was a few&nbsp;
bowls, randomly placed around the chamber.&nbsp;

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:49.080
Kayla’s torchlight fell over one of the bowls,&nbsp;
and she knelt down to take a closer look at its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:55.800
contents. It was filled with bread, they were all&nbsp;
holding different foods, dates, figs, dried fish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.840
and lentils. Of course, it wasn’t uncommon for&nbsp;
offerings to be left with the deceased during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:06.240
an Ancient Egyptian burial. But strangely, none&nbsp;
of these perishable foods seemed to have decayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:12.360
in the centuries this tomb had been lost for;&nbsp;
in fact, it all looked fresh, like it was still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.460
good enough to eat. Although the temptation to&nbsp;
actually eat any of it quickly disappeared when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:22.020
Kayla reminded herself that – while it might&nbsp;
not look it – the food down here had to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:27.120
been hundreds, maybe thousands of years old,&nbsp;
despite how perfectly preserved it appeared.&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.440
“I think I’ve got something!” Seth called&nbsp;
enthusiastically. “There’s definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.500
a story in these hieroglyphics.”
“You sure we’re in the right place?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.940
Kayla asked as she crossed the room to&nbsp;
where her colleague was. “For a tomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:43.980
it doesn’t seem like anyone was ever buried here.”
“I’ve got a theory,” he replied. “This place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:49.260
might be more of a… metaphorical tomb.”
“Right, that makes complete sense.” Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.320
sarcasm was unmistakable.
“Look, here,” he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.100
pointing to one of the drawings on the wall&nbsp;
as he spoke. It depicted a figure, laying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:01.500
dead in a square space. “I think that’s meant&nbsp;
to be this room, someone dies here, and then…”&nbsp;

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:07.500
Seth’s finger moved to another of the hieroglyphs,&nbsp;
one that Kayla recognized. It showed a being with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:12.360
the body of a man and the head of a jackal,&nbsp;
taking the dead figure down into the afterlife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:18.240
This was Anubis, one of the Ancient Egyptian&nbsp;
gods associated with the afterlife. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.100
to legends and other text gathered from the&nbsp;
pyramids in Giza, Anubis oversaw the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:28.020
the Egyptians would perform to embalm their dead,&nbsp;
preserving them for their trip to the next world.&nbsp;

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.880
“Anubis takes whoever they are to the judgment&nbsp;
hall of the afterlife to be brought before-”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.560
Seth explained, only to be cut off.
“Osiris, god of fertility, the afterlife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:42.540
and the dead,” Kayla interrupted; she had already&nbsp;
spotted the next being depicted in the glyphs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.820
a tall figure with a distinctive headpiece,&nbsp;
holding a symbolic crook and flail. “Oversaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.500
resurrection, life, probably many other things.”
“Yeah, he was a god with a few different hats,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:58.560
Seth attempted to joke. “Well, just the same one&nbsp;
hat he’s always shown wearing. Also, technically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:04.380
not a hat, the atef was a type of crown and-”
“So, hold on,” Kayla interjected again. “Didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:09.540
Osiris only bring the pharaohs in for judgment?&nbsp;
Who was this person that died? I mean the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:14.700
Egyptians never went to this amount of trouble for&nbsp;
their Average Joes, and the gods wouldn’t pay them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:20.040
any mind unless they were important somehow.”
“I’m not sure,” Seth answered earnestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:24.960
“At any rate, this could all be a myth.&nbsp;
That’s why I think this tomb is metaphorical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.060
it wasn’t built to house a dead person…”
“But to keep people believing,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:34.440
she finished his sentence for him.
He then pointed out the next part of the glyphs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:39.900
the dead figure arriving in the afterlife, carried&nbsp;
by Anubis for judgment, and then handing an object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:45.300
to Osiris. Seth explained there was a certain&nbsp;
myth surrounding Osiris and his resurrection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:50.520
a story that told of all his dead body&nbsp;
parts being found, except one. Without it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:56.220
the god could not be fully resurrected.
“My theory is that is Osiris’ ‘missing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:00.240
appendage’ as it were,” Seth’s face went&nbsp;
red as he tried to keep his composure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.460
“After our mysterious friend handed it over…”
The hieroglyphs showed the nameless dead figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:11.160
being taken from Anubis’ hand by Osiris, who then&nbsp;
placed them back in the room they had started in;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:16.620
the same chamber Seth and Kayla were standing in.&nbsp;
It seemed they’d been resurrected after death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:21.840
giving Osiris something that the god wanted&nbsp;
had earned this figure a free pass back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.640
the land of the living. The glyphs also showed&nbsp;
bowls of food around the tomb, like the ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:30.600
Kayla had found dotted around the room.
There was a type of aura drawn around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:36.060
the unknown figure now, almost like a personal&nbsp;
shield. According to the drawings and symbols&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:41.460
on the walls, and Seth’s hasty translation of&nbsp;
their meaning, this was some form of protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:47.700
granted by Osiris himself. The aura was meant&nbsp;
to prevent the figure from harm, meaning they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:52.500
couldn’t die again. However, the next portion&nbsp;
of the story showed Anubis damaging this shield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:58.020
possibly out of jealousy and spite, or perhaps&nbsp;
defying Osiris tampering with the natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:03.300
order of things by protecting the figure from&nbsp;
death. As well as causing damage to the aura of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:08.640
immortality that Osiris had bestowed on them,&nbsp;
Anubis drained the figure of all their blood.&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:14.520
“Which then caused them to go and do the same&nbsp;
to others,” Seth continued interpreting aloud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.520
“It would find people, drink&nbsp;
their blood to sustain it like-”&nbsp;

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:21.420
“Don’t say it,” Kayla sighed.
“I mean, it is a lot like a vam-”&nbsp;

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:24.960
“Egypt gave us mummies, they can’t&nbsp;
have another native monster from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:29.580
horror B-movies,” she interrupted.
“Well, maybe the legend found its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:34.920
way here somehow, or at least a more Ancient&nbsp;
Egyptian version of it,” her counterpart tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:38.760
to reason. “It looks like here, the figure&nbsp;
was far healthier after consuming blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:42.360
see how the aura around them is repaired now.”
“But, Seth, look at what they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:46.080
standing on…” Kayla whispered.
One of the final images in the glyphs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:51.780
did display exactly what Seth had described. But&nbsp;
beneath the feet of the figure, who now seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:58.200
replenished, was a mass of bodies, a pile of human&nbsp;
corpses with one person standing at the very top.&nbsp;

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:02.700
“Who’d want to live forever anyway?”&nbsp;
Kayla muttered almost inaudibly. “Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:05.580
come and have a look at these.”
Leaning over, she picked up one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:10.200
the food bowls from the floor, brimming with&nbsp;
offerings of dates. She handed it to Seth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:15.420
who marveled at how well preserved the food was.
“That’s incredible,” he remarked. “This bowl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:21.360
looks like it’s at least several thousand years&nbsp;
old, I’d guess maybe 2500 BCE. But the dates…”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.500
he picked one out of the bowl, sniffed it,&nbsp;
put it back, then did the same with another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:29.460
making sure it wasn’t a fluke. “The conditions&nbsp;
couldn’t possibly have kept them this fresh.”&nbsp;

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:35.340
“So, how is it that there are bowls of food all&nbsp;
over this room that still look edible?” she asked.&nbsp;

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:40.680
“I honestly have no-” he began to say.
The sound of heavy, grinding stone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:45.960
interrupted Seth’s sentence. Both he and Kayla&nbsp;
looked in horror as they witness the entrance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:50.940
of the tomb being sealed, the heavy stone door&nbsp;
dropping, moments away from sealing them in.&nbsp;

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:54.720
“Seth, we need to run!” Kayla urged,&nbsp;
grabbing her bag. “If we move now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:59.520
we can get out before we get shut in here.”
“But…” Seth protested, looking around the tomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:03.420
the glyph walls that had only just been&nbsp;
discovered; nobody would know that he and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:07.800
Kayla had found them if they ran now. He looked&nbsp;
around the room, pondering for a moment, his eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:11.640
darting from a bowl of bread, to the bowl of dates&nbsp;
in his hands. “We should at least take photos!”&nbsp;

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:15.120
“No time! Our lives aren’t&nbsp;
worth proving what we found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.380
better to make it out than be buried alive,”&nbsp;
Kayla shouted, darting towards the entrance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:24.300
ducking under the slowly lowering stone door.&nbsp;
“Seth, come on!” she called from the other side.&nbsp;

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:29.100
Despite her calls for him to move, her fellow&nbsp;
explorer didn’t follow. He just stood there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:33.720
watching the door get lower and the only&nbsp;
way back to the surface get narrower. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:38.160
the time it had completely closed, Kayla’s&nbsp;
voice sounded so much further away that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:42.300
Seth could barely make out what she was saying.
“I’ll get help, find some way to get you out!”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:46.380
Kayla screamed at the wall now separating&nbsp;
her from the tomb. “If you can hear me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:49.980
Seth, ration the water in your bag,&nbsp;
take small sips every few hours.”&nbsp;

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:55.800
Seth already knew the human body could survive&nbsp;
around three weeks on just water and no food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:00.540
and he trusted Kayla would have figured out how&nbsp;
to get him out by then. He gave another glance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:05.880
the food bowls scattered throughout the tomb. The&nbsp;
idea of eating any of it, knowing how old it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:11.280
potentially was, did turn his stomach a little.&nbsp;
But if it came down to having to eat just a bite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:16.500
in order to survive a little longer, Seth was&nbsp;
sure it looked fresh enough to still be edible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:21.180
It couldn’t hurt to take a bite, right?
Over the next few days, Seth’s focus was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:24.540
spent entirely on photographing and&nbsp;
transcribing more of the hieroglyphs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:29.760
occasionally stopping to take swigs of water&nbsp;
from his canteen. If anything, it was more to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:35.100
combat how warm and stuffy it was being locked&nbsp;
in a stone chamber twenty meters underground.&nbsp;

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:39.720
Meanwhile, unseen to Seth, outside Kayla&nbsp;
was working tirelessly to try and figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:45.240
out a way to get the door open. She had been&nbsp;
searching for days for any sign of some kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:50.580
switch or lever that would unseal the chamber&nbsp;
entrance. There had to be some mechanism that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:54.540
had caused the stone door to shut itself in&nbsp;
the first place, but there was nothing around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:59.280
that looked like it fit that description.
As much as she had urged Seth to abandon his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:04.620
archaeological ‘finders-keepers’ pride, Kayla was&nbsp;
hesitant to make the long journey back across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:09.360
desert to Cairo to get help. Anyone she brought&nbsp;
to the dig site might claim the discovery as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:14.100
their own, and more to the point, the more time&nbsp;
she spent traveling across the desert for help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:19.920
the more time Seth would spend trapped,&nbsp;
succumbing to gradual dehydration and starvation.&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:25.560
Little was Kayla aware; it was already too late.&nbsp;
Seth hadn’t been observing her warning about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:31.500
managing the amount he was drinking, and pretty&nbsp;
soon his canteen was empty. He cursed himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:36.480
annoyed for being so involved in his discovery&nbsp;
that he’d now potentially put his own life at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:41.760
risk. But then, he remembered the food bowls.&nbsp;
Kneeling down, he picked up the nearest one, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:46.740
same bowl with all the dates in it. Shaking his&nbsp;
head at the thought, he put them back down again.&nbsp;

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:52.260
The longer he waited, the more Seth began to&nbsp;
worry that maybe Kayla had abandoned him. And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:57.960
she had, then he was faced with the inevitability&nbsp;
that he would die down here, lost and forgotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:03.480
in an ancient tomb underground. He couldn’t help&nbsp;
but find something ironic about the whole thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:08.820
he was going to die in a tomb whose walls told a&nbsp;
tale of a dead person being brought back to life.&nbsp;

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:13.380
Maybe in another few hundred years, more&nbsp;
archaeologists would find this buried room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:18.960
and think he was the one the tomb had been&nbsp;
constructed for. Even trying to put an ironic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:24.300
spin on things wasn’t enough to give Seth much&nbsp;
peace when staring down the possibility he might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:29.880
not see the sun and walk above ground again. And&nbsp;
getting closer to death is what made him want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:35.580
avoid it, tempting him toward the bowls of food.
Every time he tried to tell himself not to touch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:42.360
any of it, the pain in his stomach swelled even&nbsp;
greater, reminding him of his own hunger. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:46.800
no clear view of the sky, Seth had little clue&nbsp;
just how many days he’d been trapped in the tomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:51.780
although he was sure it was approaching the day&nbsp;
four threshold. He couldn’t survive much longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:56.400
beyond that point without eating. As the&nbsp;
days dragged on, he abandoned his research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:01.620
and note-taking, it was impossible to focus on&nbsp;
translating hieroglyphs when he was in so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:07.620
pain, aching from the heat and having no food&nbsp;
or water, exhaustion causing him to pass out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:11.640
his body trying to conserve its&nbsp;
already limited reserves of energy.&nbsp;

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:17.280
Eventually, Seth started trying to rationalize&nbsp;
eating at least a date or two. The food hadn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:22.020
decomposed or rotted at all, even introducing&nbsp;
air from outside when the tomb was first opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:27.120
hadn’t changed the state of any of it at&nbsp;
all; not the dates, nor the bread or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:31.920
fish. It still looked fresh and edible, and&nbsp;
after God knows how long without eating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:37.980
it was beginning to look all the more appealing&nbsp;
to the starving archaeologist. Who cares if it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:44.340
been there for a thousand years or longer? If Seth&nbsp;
didn’t eat, he knew he wouldn’t last much longer.&nbsp;

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:49.740
The moment he chewed on the first date, feeling it&nbsp;
pass down his esophagus, passing from his mouth to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:55.620
his stomach, Seth immediately regretted giving in&nbsp;
to his need to eat. His mouth instantly went dry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.620 --> 00:14:00.480
far worse than it had been after this long&nbsp;
without water. It was like the tiny piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:06.360
fruit had pulled all the saliva from his mouth.&nbsp;
Seth tried to scream in pain, only to release a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:11.220
hoarse, dry-throated gasp as he felt his tongue&nbsp;
shriveling up along with his mouth and throat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:16.680
until it felt like the skin of a drum.
But it didn’t stop there, it only got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:22.620
worse. Seth stared in horror at his own arms, the&nbsp;
blue veins that ran underneath his skin rapidly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:28.320
draining away. His forearms and biceps shrunk,&nbsp;
muscle and sinews shriveling like his body had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:34.020
been left in a water evaporator. All the while,&nbsp;
the pain of having his entire physical form being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:39.300
reshaped was pure agony, the dehydration of&nbsp;
his mouth meant Seth still couldn’t scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:43.320
only drop to the ground as he felt&nbsp;
the same thing happening to his legs.&nbsp;

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:49.740
Every drop of moisture in his body, every fluid&nbsp;
from water to blood, saliva, and even urine was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:55.260
drying out, leaving Seth as little more than&nbsp;
a dusty, desiccated husk. Before his own eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.260 --> 00:15:01.380
he could only watch as his skin lost its pigment,&nbsp;
drying into a thin, leathery texture, drained of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:06.540
its original color as his blood stopped flowing.
Outside, Kayla had been coming back to the tomb’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:10.800
sealed stone door every day, and would stay&nbsp;
until the late hours of the evening. She’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:14.520
tried digging down from the surface to reach&nbsp;
the chamber where Seth had been trapped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:20.220
but it was too far underground. There was no sign&nbsp;
of any way to move or reopen the stone door… that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:24.840
is, until she heard the heavy grinding of it&nbsp;
opening on its own. Kayla raced down the steps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:30.360
towards the tomb. It had been five whole days&nbsp;
since she and Seth got separated, and he’d need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:35.700
food and medical attention. Sprinting into the&nbsp;
dark tunnel, she arrived just in time to see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:41.400
entrance reopening, expecting to see Seth standing&nbsp;
on the other side as the stone door lifted.&nbsp;

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:47.640
What she saw instead was a nightmare. It looked&nbsp;
like a corpse, an ancient and decomposed dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:52.980
body. Kayla had seen plenty entombed in sarcophagi&nbsp;
on her many travels exploring ancient ruins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:58.260
around the world. But she had never seen one&nbsp;
standing up. It was still breathing, a wheezing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:03.660
raspy sound escaping its mouth. Its skin looked&nbsp;
almost brittle as if it was stretched too thin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:08.520
almost see-through enough to show off the bones&nbsp;
underneath. The upright corpse’s face was all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:13.500
wrong too, sunken cheeks and eye sockets deeper&nbsp;
than they should have been. It looked so different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:18.660
that it wasn’t until Kayla shone her flashlight on&nbsp;
its drained face that she recognized who it was.&nbsp;

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:22.260
Seth.
He lunged at her immediately, racing towards her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:27.960
snarling with his dry, desiccated mouth. Kayla&nbsp;
turned and ran as he came after her, bony fingers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:33.240
swiping at her clothes. Her flashlight gripped in&nbsp;
her hand was all but useless as she sprinted back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:38.400
the way she’d come, swinging back and forth in her&nbsp;
arm, barely illuminating much of the cavern that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:42.780
led to the tomb. The whole while she was running&nbsp;
from it, she could hear the wheezing breaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:47.820
of Seth behind her, terrified of whatever&nbsp;
he had become, and why he was chasing her.&nbsp;

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:53.820
Then she saw it; the stairs, her one shot at&nbsp;
getting out and getting away. If she could make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:58.020
to the surface, she could put some more distance&nbsp;
between herself and the drained, dehydrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:03.120
corpse. All she had to do was make it up…
The last step, she had just made it when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:07.740
she felt the shriveled hand grip her ankle,&nbsp;
tripping her over. Falling face-first in the sand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:11.880
Kayla scrabbled at the loose grains, trying&nbsp;
to pull herself away as her body was dragged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:17.700
back down onto the stone stairs. She felt a&nbsp;
searing pain and screamed into the empty desert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:22.560
as the corpse’s fingers dug into her&nbsp;
neck, its leathery, dry skin against hers.&nbsp;

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:28.440
Eventually, the screaming stopped, replaced&nbsp;
with a dry, hoarse gasping, and then silence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:35.220
as Seth drained her body of her blood, along with&nbsp;
the rest. Pulling Kayla’s body by her ankle, Seth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:43.020
dragged what was left of her back down towards&nbsp;
the tomb, SCP-3359, to feed on what was left.&nbsp;

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:49.320
Now go and check out “SCP-1176 WHY EVERYONE WANTS&nbsp;
TO EAT THIS CORPSE” for even more mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:53.820
mummified madness. Or, if ancient temples are&nbsp;
more your thing, then why not take a visit to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.820 --> 00:18:01.020
“SCP-001 GOD’S BLIND SPOT” and uncover some&nbsp;
sinister secrets with some anomalous archaeology.

